PART 2 — THE ERGONOMIC CONTROLLER DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

This is the most important section of the entire update.

2.1 Comfort takes priority over arbitrary button assignments

Every default binding must be designed with actual controller ergonomics in mind.

Consider:

* How frequently an ability is used.
* How quickly the player must activate it.
* Whether the action is performed while moving.
* Whether the player needs precise joystick direction.
* Whether the action requires holding a button.
* Whether it is a defensive reaction.
* Whether it must be used immediately after another move.
* Whether the player needs to jump simultaneously.
* Whether a button combination is comfortable to perform.
* Whether a control forces the player to move their thumb away from another essential control.
* Whether the action can accidentally trigger another ability.
* Whether the same controls are comfortable on both PlayStation and Xbox controllers.

For example, Jump is extremely frequent and often needs to be pressed while moving or attacking.

Cross/A is therefore an appropriate default because it is easy to reach.

A basic punch is also frequent and should use an accessible face button, such as Square/X.

A special projectile may be less frequent and can comfortably use a trigger modifier combined with a face button.

However, a fast aerial attack should generally not require a complicated combination if a more comfortable single-button option is available.

Do not give a frequently used action an unnecessarily complicated input when an equally practical, more comfortable input exists.

2.2 Universal actions versus character-specific abilities

Separate actions into two conceptual categories.

Universal actions

These are shared gameplay fundamentals, such as:

* Running left and right.
* Jumping.
* Dash and air dash.
* Shield.
* Deflect.
* Fast fall.
* Pause.

Their recommended default controls should remain consistent across the entire roster.

Character-specific actions

These are the individual attacks, abilities, and special mechanics belonging to a character.

They must not automatically inherit the same physical button solely because they share an internal attack codename.

For example, attack3 might be a powerful projectile for one character and a rolling melee attack for another.

The controller input should suit the actual move, not the attack number.

Attack numbers are internal identifiers, not a controller layout philosophy.

2.3 Full player freedom

Although the default controls follow universal consistency, players must be allowed to change any gameplay binding for each individual fighter.

A player might prefer Circle/B for Jump on #0001 and Cross/A for Jump on #0002.

The customization system must allow this.

Universal consistency is a default design principle, not a limitation on customization.

2.4 Future characters

Every future fighter must be evaluated individually.

When a new character is introduced, design its default controller layout using the same comfort-first principles.

Do not automatically copy #0001 or #0002’s unique ability bindings.

Examine what the character actually does, how each ability functions, and which control would make it most comfortable.

