PART 4 — CONTROLLER CONNECTION DETECTION

4.1 Maximum of four controllers

ALVA may recognize up to four supported connected controllers simultaneously.

This is a connection-management limit, not a requirement to implement four-player combat.

The existing game primarily uses one player against a CPU.

Preserve that.

For now, one supported controller is assigned to Player 1 while additional supported controllers remain available but do not control Player 1 unless selected.

Never merge gameplay inputs from multiple controllers into one fighter.

When more than four supported controllers are connected, detect the excess and prevent additional controllers from controlling ALVA.

Use stable device assignments when possible.

4.2 Activate the Home controller button

On the Home screen, find the three small utility buttons at the top-right.

The middle button has the controller icon from controller.PNG.

This button is currently disabled.

Enable it.

Preserve its original icon, position, proportions, and visual identity.

4.3 Add a connection status indicator

Add a small circular status indicator at the bottom-right corner of the controller button.

The indicator must always represent the current recognized-controller status.

There are three statuses:

Green — Connected

A green circle with a white checkmark.

Meaning:

At least one and no more than four supported controllers are connected.

Red — Disconnected

A red circle with a clearly visible cross.

Meaning:

No supported controller is detected.

The cross should have adequate contrast. A white-outlined red cross or a white cross against the red background is acceptable if needed for visibility.

Orange — Too many controllers

An orange circle with an exclamation mark.

Meaning:

More than four supported controllers are detected.

The indicator must update automatically.

Do not display all three statuses simultaneously.

Use the existing ALVA visual language: clean, compact, modern, and consistent with the Home interface.

The status must also be available through an accessible text label.

