PART 6 — CUSTOM CIRCULAR CONTROLLER CURSOR

Add a custom circular cursor for navigating ALVA’s menus with a controller.

This should feel like a native part of the game’s interface.

6.1 Appearance

The cursor must be:

* Circular.
* Clearly visible.
* Visually consistent with ALVA.
* Smoothly animated.
* Small enough not to hide UI information.
* Large enough to identify easily.
* Distinct from the mouse cursor.

A circular outline with a subtle center indicator is a good starting design.

Use an appropriate hover and selection animation.

Avoid overly bright effects or excessive visual decoration.

6.2 Cursor controls

Default menu bindings:

Action	PlayStation	Xbox
Move cursor	Left joystick	Left joystick
Confirm	Cross	A
Back	Circle	B
Directional focus navigation	D-pad	D-pad
Scroll long panels	Right stick vertically	Right stick vertically
Pause / Resume	Options	Menu

The analog stick should move the cursor smoothly through the interface.

Use:

* Proper deadzones.
* Comfortable movement speed.
* Resolution-independent movement.
* Smooth acceleration.
* Predictable stopping.
* Viewport boundaries.
* Reliable hover detection.

The cursor should not be excessively fast or slow.

6.3 Selecting UI elements

When hovering over an interactive element, pressing Cross/A activates it.

Support:

* Home buttons.
* Mode selection.
* Difficulty selection.
* Character roster.
* Stage selection.
* Settings.
* Controller Connections.
* Controller customization.
* Dialog buttons.
* Practice menus.
* Pause menus.
* Result menus.
* Discover.
* Other navigable screens.

Only interact with enabled elements inside the currently active screen or modal scope.

Do not allow cursor clicks to pass through dialogs.

6.4 Existing navigation compatibility

ALVA already has MenuNavigator.

Integrate the circular cursor with that system.

Do not create two competing menu navigation engines.

Keep controller focus, button activation, and cursor selection synchronized.

When using the D-pad, the highlighted menu item should remain visible and automatically scroll into view.

The circular cursor may move to the focused element when directional navigation is used, provided this does not cause confusing jumps or unwanted activation.

6.5 Controller versus gameplay

The cursor appears only when the player is navigating menus or interactive overlays.

During gameplay:

* The left stick moves the character.
* The cursor disappears.
* Menu confirmation inputs cannot activate combat abilities.

During menus:

* The left stick moves the cursor.
* Combat input is suspended.
* Buttons do not accidentally activate gameplay actions.

When a menu closes, clear inappropriate buffered input to prevent accidental attacks or movement.

Mouse, keyboard, and touch must remain functional.

