The player should not be artificially restricted to the recommended default buttons.

12.4 Activation method

Players can choose appropriate activation methods.

Press: Activates once when the specified button or combination becomes active.

Hold-to-activate: Activates once after a defined short hold threshold, unless it is a genuinely continuous action.

Continuous hold: Maintains an existing held action while the input remains active.

Do not confuse holding a modifier with holding the ability itself.

For example, R2 + Square normally means hold R2 as a modifier, then press Square once.

It does not mean repeatedly firing Red while both buttons are held.

For continuous mechanics like Shield, preserve their real behavior even when remapped.

Shield must remain a held defensive action, not a toggle.

Variable-height Jump must also retain its normal duration-sensitive behavior.

Customization changes the input, not the game’s combat rules.

12.5 Press-to-bind interface

When selecting Change Binding, open an input-capture interface.

Example:

Press your preferred button or combination

The game listens to the designated controller.

Display the detected input.

Allow the player to:

* Confirm.
* Retry.
* Cancel.
* Change activation type.

Do not let captured buttons accidentally activate the menus behind the editor.

Do not perform gameplay actions while capturing bindings.

Make input capture comfortable and intuitive.

12.6 Conflict detection

Detect problematic bindings before saving.

For example:

If two ground attacks use the same single button and can activate in the same context, this is a conflict.

Show a warning identifying the conflicting actions.

However, do not treat every shared button as a conflict.

Examples of valid reuse:

* #0002’s Spin Attack and Blue Tornado share R1 because one is grounded and the other airborne.
* A basic attack and an explicitly modified version may share the same face button when the modifier logic reliably separates them.
* A ground Shield and aerial Deflect may use the same button.

Allow legitimate contextual reuse.

Prevent configurations that create unavoidable ambiguity.

If necessary, provide a way to restore the original controls.

12.7 Visual controller diagram

Include an interactive controller diagram within the customization interface if practical.

Show the currently recognized PlayStation or Xbox layout.

Highlight the buttons involved in the selected binding.

For combinations, highlight both inputs.

This would make customizing controls easier to understand.

Keep the diagram lightweight and use appropriate original or permitted vector UI assets.

Do not make the diagram mandatory for functionality; the editor must remain accessible through textual controls and keyboard navigation.

