PART 16 — IMPORTANT TECHNICAL CORRECTIONS

16.1 Do not make controller inputs feel delayed

A player must never feel that basic attacks, Jump, Shield, or movement respond slowly because the system is waiting to detect a possible button combination.

Prefer clear modifier-first controls.

Recognize simultaneous inputs when unambiguous, but do not impose substantial artificial delays on ordinary actions.

Responsiveness is part of comfort.

16.2 Avoid accidental actions

Never trigger multiple combat abilities from one intended combination.

Do not allow trigger release to activate an unmodified attack.

Do not fire attacks repeatedly from ordinary held buttons.

Do not allow menu navigation inputs to leak into combat.

16.3 Support controller disconnections

If the active controller disconnects, immediately release its held gameplay inputs.

Prevent:

* Endless running.
* Permanent Shield.
* Stuck attacks.
* Unintended Dash requests.
* Stuck menu selection.

Allow the player to select another connected supported controller.

Keyboard input should remain available.

16.4 Correct controller identification

Browsers may hide gamepads until initial interaction.

Check existing connections where permitted, react to events, and provide manual refresh.

Do not assume a gamepad is unsupported merely because a browser has not exposed it yet.

Do not misidentify uncertain devices.

Clearly communicate detection limitations.

16.5 Do not confuse four connections with multiplayer

This update supports detection and management of up to four controllers.

It must not silently implement multiplayer modes or alter Quick Battle’s player-versus-CPU structure.

One active gameplay controller controls Player 1.

16.6 Handle joystick direction carefully

Directional attacks must use sensible thresholds.

For #0002:

* Left/right + Square → Homing Attack.
* Down + Square → Bounce Attack.
* Neutral + Square → Homing Attack using facing.

When the joystick points diagonally, use a consistent dominant-axis policy rather than randomly selecting a move.

Test directions near deadzone boundaries.

