FINAL INSTRUCTIONS — COMFORT ABOVE EVERYTHING

The fundamental purpose of this update is controller comfort.

Do not treat comfort as a secondary detail.

ALVA is a fast-paced platform fighter. Players must be able to move, jump, defend, Dash, and perform their character’s abilities naturally.

A successful controller layout should feel intuitive after a short period of play.

Players should not need to memorize awkward combinations for ordinary attacks.

Fast abilities should be accessible quickly.

Movement-oriented actions should not unnecessarily interfere with joystick movement.

Defensive reactions should be reliable and immediate.

Powerful or less frequent abilities may use comfortable modifier combinations when appropriate.

Character-specific controls should reflect how each fighter actually plays.

Do not force every fighter into the same ability layout.

For example:

* Both characters use Square/X for their primary punch because it is comfortable and consistent.
* #0001 uses R2/RT combinations for projectiles because that groups its special attacks logically.
* #0002 uses directional Square/X combinations for its aerial Homing and Bounce Attacks because those actions are direction-dependent.
* #0002 uses R1/RB for both ground Spin Attack and aerial Blue Tornado because their ground/air contexts make this intuitive and conflict-free.
* All fighters use L1/LB for Dash by default because it allows comfortable movement while keeping the right thumb available for attacks.
* All fighters use Circle/B for defensive actions by default, with ground Shield held and aerial Deflect activated by a fresh press.

These are examples of the reasoning that should guide this entire update.

The default controller bindings must be deliberately designed rather than mechanically assigned.

At the same time, players must have complete freedom to build layouts that match their individual preferences.

The ideal result combines three qualities:

1. Universal consistency: Shared gameplay fundamentals feel familiar across characters.

2. Character-specific comfort: Each unique ability is assigned an input that naturally suits its mechanics.

3. Complete customization: Players can override the recommended layout independently for every fighter.

Do not sacrifice any of these qualities.

Prioritize real gameplay comfort, input responsiveness, and predictability over unnecessary complexity.

Preserve the existing mechanics, architecture, and identity of ALVA.

Implement the update fully, test it carefully, and deliver an integrated system rather than disconnected features.

When finished, report the implementation changes, automated test results, controller-specific verification performed, and any limitations.

The final result should make playing ALVA with a controller feel natural, comfortable, responsive, and intentionally designed for every character — including the characters added in the future.
